# Migration files are hashed byte for byte, so their line endings are load-bearing.
#
# scripts/migrate.mjs stores the SHA-256 of each migration.sql and compares it on
# every run — the same thing Prisma does. With core.autocrlf=true, git rewrites
# these files to CRLF on checkout, which changes that hash and makes every
# already-applied migration report DRIFT. A tool that warns about all of them
# warns about none of them, so a real edit would go unnoticed.
#
# Pinned to LF so the bytes are the same on every machine and in every clone.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Shell scripts run in Alpine, where CRLF is a syntax error rather than
# whitespace: `set -e` followed by a carriage return is read as `set -e<CR>`
# and the shell answers `illegal option`. With core.autocrlf=true - which is
# the default on Windows and is set on this machine - git rewrites these on
# checkout, so the entrypoint was correct in the repository, correct in the
# image I built from a working copy that predated the checkout, and broken in
# every fresh clone. Pinned to LF for the same reason the migrations are.
*.sh text eol=lf
